# =============================================================================
# Enterprise AI Gateway — .gitignore
# =============================================================================
# This file prevents accidental commits of credentials, generated artifacts,
# editor state, and OS noise. Every section is annotated with its rationale.
#
# SECURITY NOTE: Never commit secrets, keys, tokens, or kubeconfigs.
# If a secret is accidentally committed, treat it as compromised immediately:
#   1. Rotate the credential.
#   2. Use `git filter-repo` to purge it from history.
#   3. Force-push and notify all collaborators.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# PYTHON
# Excludes compiled bytecode and virtual environment directories.
# Bytecode is machine-specific and regenerated automatically; committing it
# causes spurious diffs and cross-platform compatibility problems.
# Virtual environments contain OS-specific binaries and should be recreated
# from requirements files, not checked into source control.
# =============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/
env/
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
*.egg


# =============================================================================
# VS CODE
# Editor workspace settings and extension state are personal to each developer.
# Committing .vscode/ overrides teammates' preferences and can expose local
# paths or extension configurations.
# Exception: .vscode/extensions.json (recommended extensions) and
# .vscode/settings.json (project-level formatter settings) may be committed
# by choice — remove them from this pattern if your team uses them.
# =============================================================================
.vscode/
*.code-workspace


# =============================================================================
# AWS
# Prevents committing AWS credentials, SSH private keys, and kubeconfig files.
# An accidentally committed AWS credential can result in account takeover
# within minutes (automated scanners monitor public repositories in real time).
# kubeconfig files contain cluster API server addresses and authentication
# tokens that grant kubectl access to the cluster.
# =============================================================================
.aws/
*.pem
*.key
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
aws-exports.js
.aws-sam/


# =============================================================================
# KUBERNETES — SECRETS
# Kubernetes Secret manifests contain base64-encoded credentials.
# Base64 is encoding, not encryption — it provides zero security.
# Patterns below catch common naming conventions for secret files.
# The canonical secrets reference for this repo is litellm/secrets-example.yaml
# (contains only placeholders, safe to commit).
# =============================================================================
*.secret.yaml
*.secret.yml
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
*-secret.yaml
*-secret.yml
*-secrets.yaml
*-secrets.yml


# =============================================================================
# TERRAFORM
# State files contain plaintext representations of all managed infrastructure,
# including sensitive values (passwords, private IPs, resource IDs).
# The .terraform/ directory contains provider binaries and cached modules —
# large, platform-specific, and fully reproducible via `terraform init`.
# Use a remote backend (S3 + DynamoDB, or Terraform Cloud) for state storage.
# =============================================================================
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.backup
*.tfstate.lock.info
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json


# =============================================================================
# OPERATING SYSTEMS
# OS-generated metadata files serve no purpose in a shared repository and
# cause noise in pull requests for developers on different platforms.
# =============================================================================

# macOS
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# Windows
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
ehthumbs_vista.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk

# Linux
*~


# =============================================================================
# LOGS
# Log files grow unboundedly and contain runtime-specific data (timestamps,
# PIDs, request IDs) that differs on every run. They are runtime artifacts,
# not source code. Ship logs to CloudWatch or your observability backend.
# =============================================================================
*.log
*.log.*
logs/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*


# =============================================================================
# ENVIRONMENT FILES
# .env files frequently contain API keys, database URLs, and other secrets.
# They must never be committed, even in development.
# Use litellm/secrets-example.yaml as the reference for expected variables.
# The only safe pattern: commit a .env.example with placeholder values only.
# =============================================================================
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample


# =============================================================================
# TEMPORARY FILES
# Scratch directories and editor swap files are transient working state.
# =============================================================================
tmp/
temp/
*.tmp
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.cache/
